# rules: swift-keychain-readable-when-locked
# languages: swift
# fixture: swift/CWE-311.swift
# from: CWE-311 (Missing Encryption of Sensitive Data), CWE-312, MASVS-STORAGE-1
#
# A keychain item's protection class is the only thing that decides when it can
# be decrypted, and `kSecAttrAccessibleAlways` says "whenever the device is
# powered on". That includes while it is locked in somebody else's hand, and it
# includes while it is being restored from an unencrypted backup onto a
# different phone - so the item stops being protected by the passcode, which
# was the whole of its protection.
#
# Apple deprecated both `Always` spellings in iOS 12 and the compiler has said
# so since. The rule reports them anyway, because a deprecation warning in a
# build log is not a finding anybody reads, and because the shape it warns
# about is the one that matters: `kSecAttrAccessibleWhenUnlocked` needs the
# passcode and `kSecAttrAccessibleAfterFirstUnlock` needs it once per boot,
# and either is a decision. `Always` is the absence of one.
#
# Both spellings of the write are read, because the query is built either way
# in real code: as an entry in the dictionary literal, and as a subscript
# assignment onto a dictionary built a line earlier.
#
# The key is written out rather than held in a hole, and that is not tidiness.
# A dictionary literal is one node, so a pattern rooted at it binds its *first*
# entry and no other - the Go pass learned the same thing about a composite
# literal's fields. Naming `kSecAttrAccessible` is what lets the pattern reach
# the entry wherever in the literal it stands.

[ "kSecAttrAccessible as String: $V",
  "kSecAttrAccessible: $V",
  "$D[kSecAttrAccessible as String] = $V",
  "$D[kSecAttrAccessible] = $V" ] as $protection
| "^kSecAttrAccessibleAlways" as $whileLocked
| scan_ast("*.swift"; $protection) as $all
| ( $all | of($protection) | where_capture("V"; $whileLocked) | focus("V") )
| finding("swift-keychain-readable-when-locked";
    "$V lets this item be read while the phone is locked and out of a backup "
    + "taken from it; kSecAttrAccessibleWhenUnlockedThisDeviceOnly needs the "
    + "passcode")
| report
